 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gaussian Rasterization Configuration Setup](10752-gaussian-rasterization-configuration-setup.md)
-Initializes the `GaussianRasterizationSettings` object and prepares auxiliary state tensors required to invoke the differentiable Gaussian rasterizer. It translates high-level camera parameters (Field of View) and scene constraints into the specific numerical formats expected by the CUDA kernel, handling state and memory layout rather than pixel-wise projection.
+Initializes the `GaussianRasterizationSettings` object and prepares auxiliary state tensors required to invoke the differentiable Gaussian rasterizer. It translates high-level camera parameters (Field of View) and scene constraints into the specific numerical formats expected by the CUDA kernel, handling state and memory layout rather than pixel-wise projection. Choose it when setting up a training loop or inference pipeline to ensure the rasterizer receives correctly formatted tensors.
 
 ### [GSCache DiffRasterizer Interface](18374-gscache-diffrasterizer-interface.md)
-A C++ class-based abstraction for managing the differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians. It encapsulates camera state, frame buffer management, and the forward/backward passes required for gradient-based optimization, providing a unified interface that integrates optimization steps (densification, pruning) and data access for Gaussian model parameters.
+A C++ class-based abstraction for managing the differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians. It encapsulates camera state, frame buffer management, and the forward/backward passes required for gradient-based optimization, providing a unified interface that integrates optimization steps (densification, pruning) and data access for Gaussian model parameters. Choose it when building a production-grade training framework that requires explicit memory management and optimization integration.
+
+### [Restricting Gaussian Kernel Support](10323-restricting-gaussian-kernel-support.md)
+A rendering optimization technique that reduces the spatial footprint of Gaussian primitives to minimize superfluous ray-Gaussian intersections and accelerate volume rendering. It achieves this by replacing the standard Gaussian kernel with a generalized exponent form and applying adaptive alpha clamping to zero out contributions below a specific threshold. Unlike standard splatting which uses fixed bounding boxes or infinite support approximations, this method dynamically adjusts the effective radius based on the kernel's shape parameters and opacity. Choose it when rendering scenes with high Gaussian density to reduce overdraw and improve throughput.
+
+### [Gaussian Rasterization (Forward)](17476-gaussian-rasterization-forward.md)
+A differentiable rasterization method that projects 3D Gaussians onto a 2D image plane to produce a rendered image and per-Gaussian screen-space radii, supporting both precomputed 3D covariances and scale/rotation parameterizations. It differs from standard rasterization by handling continuous volumetric primitives via alpha blending, enabling gradient flow through the rendering process for optimization. Choose it as the core forward pass when training differentiable Gaussian splatting models.
+
+### [3D Gaussian Splatting with Feature Splatting](19779-3d-gaussian-splatting-with-feature-splatting.md)
+Extends the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering pipeline to handle high-dimensional semantic feature tensors alongside RGB color, enabling high-fidelity point cloud rendering where both appearance and semantic information are preserved. It differs from standard 3DGS by modifying the rasterization kernel to accumulate and differentiate multi-channel feature maps in addition to color, allowing for downstream tasks such as semantic segmentation or feature-based reconstruction. Choose it when multi-modal or semantic-aware rendering is required.
+
+### [DOFGS Rendering Pipeline](21470-dofgs-rendering-pipeline.md)
+Extends standard 3D Gaussian Splatting by introducing differentiable depth-of-field (DoF) effects, allowing for the simulation of camera defocus blur during rasterization. It solves the problem of realistic optical blur in neural radiance fields by treating focal distance and aperture size as learnable parameters, enabling joint optimization of scene geometry and camera optics. It differs from standard Gaussian Splatting by computing a Circle of Confusion (CoC) alpha channel and applying a spatially varying blur kernel based on depth, rather than a uniform opacity mask. Choose it when photorealistic camera optics and depth-of-field simulation are needed.
 
 ### 3D Half-Gaussian Rasterization
-A rasterization technique that represents a 3D Gaussian kernel as two halves split by a plane through its center, allowing distinct opacity values for each half to better represent sharp edges and planar surfaces [S7]. The splitting plane normal $\mathbf{n} = [n_1, n_2, n_3]^T$ is a learned unit vector, stored using the three unused normal parameters in the original 3D-GS kernel implementation [S7]. Opacity $\alpha_1$ applies where $\mathbf{n}^T(\mathbf{x}-\boldsymbol{\mu}) \ge 0$, and $\alpha_2$ applies where $\mathbf{n}^T(\mathbf{x}-\boldsymbol{\mu}) < 0$ [S7]. This increases memory by only one additional parameter per kernel compared to 3D-GS [S7].
